qfield('hcc','HCC-D16','question','Decide how SBRT bridging is selected, considering the differing nonrandomized comparative cohorts, and name who adjudicates bridging modality choice.','B11-R13-CF02',src)
qfield('hcc','HCC-D16','divergence','Comparative bridging cohorts differ in selection and design. Toronto reserved SBRT for patients unsuitable for other options; the later Hong Kong prospective SBRT cohort with historical controls reported more complete pathological responses and less dropout. These comparisons do not establish one universally superior bridge.','B11-R13-CF02',src)
qfield('hcc','HCC-D16','rationale','Toronto reported explant complete necrosis of 13.3% after SBRT, 24.3% after TACE and 49.2% after RFA, with unequal patient selection. Wong 2021 (PMID 34091914; abstract-level verification) reported 48.1%, 25% and 17.9% after prospective SBRT versus historical TACE/HIFU. Different selection and comparators prevent a causal ranking; modality choice requires transplant and radiation review.','B11-R13-CF02',src)
# Pre-LT and post-LT immune checkpoint evidence are separate.
src='Full_Text_Review_2026-10-31/A_Reconciliation_2026-10-31/B_R1_Reconciliation.md B-028/B-029; AASLD HCC p20; Kayali36102312 post-LT population'
old='No washout interval has been derived. The whole evidence base is 52 patients pooled from case reports and small series, a median of 1 patient per publication: acute graft rejection in 15 of 52 (28.8%), and 7 patients (13.4% of the cohort, 7 of the 15 who rejected) died of liver failure from rejection. Estimated mean overall survival was 3.5 months (95% CI 1.6 to 5.4) with rejection versus 17.2 months (12.1 to 22.2) without, and 46.7% of those who rejected died within one month. No interval from transplantation predicted rejection (AUROC 0.373, P = 0.170), immunosuppression could not be analysed because regimens were reported inconsistently, and the PD-L1 biopsy signal rests on 9 biopsies. Publication bias runs in both directions.'
replace(old,'Kayali’s 52-patient pooled review concerns checkpoint inhibitors given after transplantation and cannot define a pre-transplant washout. Pre-LT cohorts provide observational timing evidence. AASLD advises discontinuation at least three months before LT, but no universally safe interval is validated and rejection can still occur. Transplant-specific review must document the interval, prior immune toxicity, alternatives and immunosuppression plan before exposure.','B-028;B-029',src,'hcc')
# pCCA adjuvant attribution and nodal treatment routing.
src=B+'B12_hilar_roadmap/reviews/R12/R12_REPORT.md F01–F03; Milan Rec9/53/54 pp74,79; BILCAP Fig4'
